WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
Pull up a chair. This is Models and Agents Episode 71 for June 5th, 2026.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.420
Your daily AI briefing. Plenty of model releases and agent benchmarks moving today.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.880
Microsoft just dropped seven new MAI models purpose-built for reasoning,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:20.360
coding, image, voice, and transcription. All integrated into the Microsoft stack.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.980
The headline model is MAI Thinking One, a medium-sized system trained from scratch on clean data

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.400
that matches leading models on software engineering benchmarks.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:40.140
In blind evaluations, it is preferred to Sonnet 4.6. MAI code One Flash uses 5 billion active

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.840
parameters and targets GitHub co-pilot and versus code integration while running cheaper than

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:53.600
comparable models. MAI Image 2.5 and its Flash variant claim to surpass Nano Banana Pro on Arena

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.140
scores for text-to-image and editing tasks. MAI Transcribe 1.5 delivers state-of-the-art

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:07.880
accuracy across 43 languages at five times the speed of competitors. MAI Voice 2 supports 15 languages

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:13.940
and adapts from short-voice samples. Open-weight releases of the coding and reasoning variants are

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:19.880
still under product terms rather than permissive licenses. Builders inside the Microsoft ecosystem

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:27.800
should test MAI code hyphen One, hyphen Flash in co-pilot workflows this week to compare cost

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:34.400
and latency against current HIKU class options. This release continues the frontier model's arc where

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.900
closed systems trade capability and price leads and it raises the open question of whether the

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:45.940
next cadence of releases will keep narrowing cost per token. Anthropic reported that its engineers

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:54.080
now ship eight times more code per quarter than in 2021 through 2025. On open-ended coding problems,

00:01:54.340 --> 00:02:01.500
cloud reached a 76% success rate, a 50-point jump in six months. Many internal engineers say

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.260
cloud code quality now matches human parity. With expectations, it will exceed that threshold within

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:13.600
the year. The roughly three times average speed up previously reported for cloud Opus 4 was corrected

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:21.340
to date from May 2025, not 2024. Teams evaluating coding assistance should run the same open-ended

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:27.440
problem set against cloud and current alternatives this week to measure the gap. This development

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:33.180
fits the ongoing reasoning model's conversation by showing measurable velocity gains rather than just

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:39.600
benchmark lifts and it leaves open whether those gains translate to real-world value outside internal

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:47.780
workflows. Superlabs released Super-50M reasoning, a 50 million parameter model fine-tuned for six

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:56.980
epochs on a 500 gigabyte part. Sign-percent synthetic data set generated by 231.7B. Every response

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:03.400
follows a strict begin of thought to end of thought, then begin of solution to end of solution format.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:09.920
The model is fully open, experimental and known to hallucinate. It is the first reasoning model from

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:17.920
the Super-50M collection under Project Camira, with 124,350,000,000 variants planned next.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.940
This sits in the open-weight models track where families are narrowing the gap with closed frontier

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:30.200
systems, though licensing terms remain a key open question for commercial use. An open AI reasoning

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:36.420
model produced a counter example to an 80-year-old Erdos conjecture. Researchers Alex Wei,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:45.780
Hongshun Wu, and WJMZBMR1 discuss the discovery on the open AI podcast, highlighting collaboration

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:52.340
between mathematicians and models. The episode is available on Spotify, Apple podcasts, and YouTube.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.800
Watch the next frontier reasoning releases for similar mathematical discovery workflows.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:04.500
Open AI began rolling out a new memory system to plus and pro users in the United States

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:10.240
that automatically tracks important details and provides a memory summary for review and control.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:16.640
Users receive two times more memory capacity and can revert to legacy-saved memories and settings.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:23.420
The update requires the latest iOS or Android app and will expand to additional plans and countries

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:30.100
soon. Test the memory summary interface on multi-session projects to see how context persistence changes

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:37.480
daily workflows. Shell is extending its C3 AI reliability suite deployment to more than 30,000

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:43.080
pieces of equipment with new autonomous agents that move from anomaly detection to fully automated

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:49.960
predictive maintenance. The agents operate across upstream and downstream operations. Monitor how

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:56.000
Shell measures reduction in unplanned downtime once the agents are live. This update advances the

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.040
agents and tool use track by moving from detection to closed loop action, leaving the open question

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:07.960
of long horizon reliability still in focus. Perplexity AI announced a hybrid inference

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.780
orchestrator for personal computers that automatically routes tasks between on device and cloud models.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.480
The system decides routing without user intervention. Developers building local first

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:26.020
agents should watch for the open release or API to test automatic tasks splitting and video release

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:33.440
dynamo snapshot a CRIU based system that checkpoints and restores VLLM inference workers on Kubernetes

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:42.280
using kudot checkpoint tools. The goal is fast start up for AI inference workloads. Teams running VLLM

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:47.320
on Kubernetes should test snapshot restart times against cold starts in their current clusters.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.880
This sits in the AI compute and inference conversation where the cost curve keeps falling,

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:57.700
though compute supply constraints remain the larger open question for production teams.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:06.660
I use our posted detailed llama dot cpp benchmarks comparing the RTX pro 4500 blackwell 32

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:14.520
gigabyte card against the RTX 5000 60 Ti 16 gigabyte across dense and mixture of experts models.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:22.100
Prompt processing improved 1.9 to 6 times and token generation improved 1.6 to 2.6 times,

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:29.920
depending on model size and quantization. The card runs at 200 watts versus the 5,000 90s 400

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:36.940
to 600 watts while delivering professional features including ECC memory. Builders choosing between

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:42.840
consumer and pro blackwell cards for 24 seven local inference should review the full table for their

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:52.340
target models. A new open source tool under MIT license creates optimized NVFP4 and MXFP6 GGUF

00:06:52.340 --> 00:07:00.100
files directly from BF16 sources using i matrix and KLD data. It supports layer by layer candidate

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:09.240
selection, RSF scale fitting and tensor promotion between NVFP4 and MXFP6. The project includes a

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:15.920
text UI wizard and skills and agents markdown files for AI assisted use. Clone the repo and run

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:27.480
on a single 5,090 to reproduce the latest 23.6 27B NVFP4 MTP GGUF V3. Okay, let's pop the hood on

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:33.840
the NVIDIA Dynamo snapshot approach. Everyone talks about fast startup for inference services as if

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:41.160
it is just a matter of caching weights. In practice, restoring a full VLLM worker involves capturing

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:48.140
GPU state, CUDA context and KV cache contents that are tightly coupled to specific devices.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:56.620
CRIU plus CUDA checkpoint solves this by freezing the process tree and GPU memory mappings into a

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:02.660
portable snapshot that can be restored on the same or compatible hardware. The engineering trade-off

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:10.140
is that snapshot size grows with batch size and context length. A 128kg context worker can produce

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:17.100
multi-gigabyte images even after compression. Restore latency drops from tens of seconds to sub-second

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:23.220
in the best case, but only when the target node has identical driver versions and sufficient free VRAM.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:29.320
The gacha that bites most teams is that any change to model weights or tokenizer invalidates

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:36.080
prior snapshots, forcing a full cold start. Use this technique when you need rapid scale up behind

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.880
an autoscaler and can tolerate the storage overhead. Fall back to standard container polls when

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:48.620
model iteration is frequent. Run the super 50m reasoning inference script on a short factual prompt

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:56.040
to see the explicit thought then-solution format in action. Test MAI-2-flash-1-flash inside

00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:02.300
GitHub co-pilot or versus code if you have Microsoft stack access and compare cost per token

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:08.440
against current high-coo class options. Apply the new chat GPT memory summary on a multi-day

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:13.620
coding project and review what it automatically retains versus your manual saved memories.

